{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Usability Testing and Learning Evidence","title":"Prioritizing Iterations","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting prioritizing iterations to storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_usability_testing_and_learning_evidence","ail606_supplement_prioritizing_iterations","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":7,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"05","section":"07","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/05/06"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_5_7_prioritizing_iterations_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_5_7_prioritizing_iterations_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_5_7_prioritizing_iterations_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_5_7_prioritizing_iterations_calibration"]},"content":"# Prioritizing Iterations\n\n![Prioritizing Iterations textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/05-07-prioritizing-iterations.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA test usually surfaces more problems than a team can fix before the next build, so the real skill is not finding issues but deciding which ones to fix first. Teams default to fixing what is easy (a typo, a color) because easy work feels like progress, while the issue actually blocking learners sits untouched because it is hard. This section is about ranking revisions by their effect on the learning goal rather than by their cost to fix, using severity (how badly an issue blocks the goal) and reach (how many learners hit it) instead of effort. Prioritization is where formative testing either pays off or is wasted, because the same evidence can drive a build that removes the worst barrier or one that polishes around it.\n\nIn AIL 606 the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes is revised across iterations, and the order of those revisions is itself a defensible decision. A prioritized backlog is an argument: it says these problems, in this order, because of this evidence.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Rank issues by severity and reach against the learning goal, not by ease of fixing.\n- Treat accessibility barriers as non-negotiable even when they do not block the median learner.\n- Build a triage table that makes the ordering inspectable and arguable.\n- Defend an iteration plan by tracing each item's position to its effect on the goal.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA test of a tutorial storyboard turns up four issues. The team's instinct is to start with the welcome-screen typo and the button color, because both are five-minute fixes, and to \"get to the rest later.\" Later never comes before the deadline, and the next build ships with the same blocking defect it had before: three of five participants could not find the Submit control and abandoned the task.\n\nThe plan optimized for motion, not impact. It cleared the trivial and left the barrier that actually stops people from reaching the learning goal. The team will demo a tidier interface and report progress, but the one number that matters (whether learners can complete the task) will not have moved, because the iteration spent its budget on the wrong end of the list.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nPrioritization runs on two axes that effort routinely overrides: severity and reach. Severity asks how much an issue blocks the goal; reach asks how many learners encounter it. Ranking by their combination, rather than by how cheap the fix is, is the entire move.\n\n| Axis | Question | Why it ranks ahead of effort |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Severity | Does it block the learning goal? | A blocking defect wastes the whole session for that learner |\n| Reach | How many learners hit it? | A widespread issue compounds across the audience |\n| Effort | How hard to fix? | Real, but it sets order only among equally important items |\n\nThere is one principled exception to \"rank by impact on the median learner,\" and it is an access barrier. A low-contrast control may not block the typical user, but for a learner with low vision it is a wall, and **UDL learner variability** treats that as non-negotiable: access is not a nice-to-have that competes on severity-times-reach, it is a floor below which the design cannot ship. So an accessibility issue can outrank a cosmetic one even when its raw reach looks modest, because the learners it excludes are excluded completely.\n\nScore an issue by severity times reach and watch how effort never enters the ranking. Move the sliders for one issue to see why a high-severity, high-reach defect outranks a cheap cosmetic fix no matter how fast the cosmetic one is to ship.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Severity x reach priority score (effort excluded by design)\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"severity\",\"label\":\"Severity (1 trivial to 5 blocks the goal)\",\"min\":1,\"max\":5,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"pts\",\"default\":5},{\"name\":\"reach\",\"label\":\"Participants who hit it\",\"min\":0,\"max\":10,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"of 10\",\"default\":3},{\"name\":\"effort\",\"label\":\"Effort to fix (1 quick to 5 hard)\",\"min\":1,\"max\":5,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"pts\",\"default\":4}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Priority score (severity x reach)\",\"unit\":\"pts\",\"expr\":\"severity * reach\"},{\"label\":\"Reach as share of audience\",\"unit\":\"%\",\"expr\":\"100 * reach / 10\"},{\"label\":\"Effort (records order among equals only)\",\"unit\":\"pts\",\"expr\":\"effort\"}]}'></parameter-explorer>\n\nSet severity to 5 and reach to 3 for a blocking Submit-findability defect, then a trivial typo at severity 1 and reach 1: the priority scores are 15 versus 1, and the effort slider changes neither. Effort is real, but it only breaks ties among items of equal priority; let it lead and you sort the backlog by comfort. **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)** reminds you that the goal is a learning performance, so an issue is severe to the degree it blocks that performance, not to the degree it annoys someone. And **[cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load)** explains why a \"small\" friction can rank high: a confusing control that adds extraneous load at a decision point can derail the learning task even if it never produces an outright failure, which is why hesitation data, not just abandonment data, feeds the severity judgment.\n\n## Worked Example: Prioritizing Which Issues to Fix First\n\nA test surfaces four issues. The team must decide what to revise before the next build.\n\n**Before (fix by ease, not impact):**\n\n> Plan: Start with the typo on the welcome screen and the button color, because they are quick. We'll get to the rest later.\n\nWalk through a defensible prioritization, step by step:\n\n1. **List each issue with severity and reach.** Severity = how much it blocks the goal; reach = how many participants hit it.\n\n| Issue | Hit by | Blocks task? | Severity |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Cannot find Submit; 3 abandoned | 3 of 5 | Yes | High |\n| Quiz feedback misread as error | 2 of 5 | Partly | Medium |\n| Button color low contrast | 4 of 5 | No | Low (but accessibility) |\n| Welcome-screen typo | 1 of 5 | No | Trivial |\n\n2. **Sort by severity x reach, not by effort.** The Submit issue blocks the core task for most users; it ranks first.\n3. **Protect access as a non-negotiable.** Low contrast does not block the task but is a **UDL learner variability** access barrier, so it stays above the typo.\n4. **Schedule the cheap-but-trivial last.**\n\n**After (impact-ordered plan):**\n\n> Next iteration, in order: (1) fix Submit findability (high severity, 3 of 5 blocked); (2) relabel quiz feedback (medium); (3) raise button contrast (accessibility); (4) typo, if time allows.\n\nThe after version is defensible because effort is spent where it removes the largest barrier to the learning goal, and access is not deprioritized. The before version optimized for what was easy, leaving the task-blocking defect unaddressed, the classic prioritization trap.\n\nOrder the four issues from the worked example, applying the rule that severity times reach leads, accessibility is a floor that cannot be traded down, and the cheap-but-trivial fix comes last.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Sequence the next iteration from four surfaced issues\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Which issue must lead the backlog?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Welcome-screen typo (1 of 5, blocks nothing)\",\"Cannot find Submit; 3 of 5 abandoned the task\",\"Low-contrast button (4 of 5, blocks no one)\",\"Quiz feedback misread (2 of 5, partly blocks)\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Submit findability is high severity (blocks the core task) at high reach (3 of 5). Highest severity times reach against the learning goal, so it leads.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The low-contrast button blocks no one yet is hit by 4 of 5. Where does it sit?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Last, because it blocks no one\",\"Dead last, below the typo\",\"Above the typo, because it is an accessibility floor\",\"First, because reach is highest\"],\"answerIndex\":2,\"reveal\":\"Contrast is an access barrier under UDL learner variability: for a low-vision learner it is total exclusion, not friction. Access is a floor, so it stays above the trivial typo even though it does not block the median learner.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Final order: (1) fix Submit findability (high severity, 3 of 5 blocked); (2) relabel quiz feedback (medium, partial block); (3) raise button contrast (accessibility floor); (4) typo, if time allows. Read back, each position cites the evidence that earned it, never the hour it takes to fix.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\n<inline-check question=\"A missing caption on a key diagram blocks deaf learners entirely but only some learners hit it; an off-brand color is noticed by one stakeholder and blocks nothing. Which leads, and why?\" explanation=\"The missing caption is an access floor under UDL learner variability: for the learners it affects it is total exclusion, not mild friction, so it cannot be traded down on raw reach. The color blocks nothing and drops to last. Access barriers are non-negotiable even when their reach looks modest.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The color, because more stakeholders will notice it\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The caption, because access is a floor and exclusion outranks a cosmetic issue\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Whichever is cheaper to fix first\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Neither; both are low reach so both wait\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nOrder by severity times reach against the learning goal, hold accessibility as a floor, and let effort break ties only among items of equal importance. If the first item on your backlog is the cheapest rather than the most blocking, the list is sorted by comfort, not evidence.\n\nBefore locking the iteration plan into your storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes, check three things:\n\n1. **Impact check:** Is each item ranked by its effect on the learning goal, not by how easy it is?\n2. **Access check:** Have accessibility barriers been protected as non-negotiable, regardless of raw reach?\n3. **Traceability check:** Can you point to the evidence (severity and reach) behind each item's position?\n\nIf any check fails, the iteration will spend its budget on motion rather than on the barrier that matters.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common error is letting effort masquerade as priority, so the backlog becomes a list of quick wins with the hard, important problem perpetually at the bottom. A second error is treating accessibility as a low-priority polish item because it does not block the median learner, which silently accepts excluding the learners it does block. A third is ranking by how loudly an issue was reported rather than by how much it blocks the goal, which lets one vivid complaint jump the queue ahead of a quiet, widespread barrier.\n\nA backlog sorted by ease is a backlog that protects the team's comfort at the learner's expense.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nTrace one backlog from instinct to a defensible order. A storyboard test surfaces a frame whose narration runs long (annoying, two participants mentioned it), a missing caption on a key diagram (blocks deaf learners entirely), and a slightly off-brand color (one stakeholder noticed). The effort instinct fixes the color first; the impact discipline does not. The missing caption is an access floor under **UDL learner variability**, so it leads even though only some learners hit it, because for them it is total exclusion, not mild friction.\n\nThe long narration ranks next, not because it was mentioned, but because **[cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load)** flags overlong audio as extraneous load competing with the diagram the frame is teaching, so it plausibly blocks the learning action for more than the two who complained. The off-brand color, blocking nothing, drops to last. Read back, the order is an argument anyone can check: caption (access floor), narration (load on the learning goal), color (cosmetic), and each position cites the evidence that earned it rather than the hour it takes to fix.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read an iteration plan, keep one column for each item's rank and one for the justification, then flag any item whose position is explained by effort rather than by severity, reach, or access. Those are the places the plan has quietly sorted itself by convenience, and they are where the next build will disappoint.\n\nA strong reader of a prioritization can answer four questions:\n\n1. Is each item ranked by its effect on the learning goal?\n2. Were accessibility barriers held as a floor rather than traded against severity?\n3. What evidence (how many, how blocking) sits behind each position?\n4. Where did ease of fixing distort the order?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe iteration plan is strong when a reviewer can read the ordering, ignore how hard each fix is, and still agree the sequence removes the largest barriers to learning first. Treat the ranking justification as the load-bearing element; an order without cited severity and reach is an assertion, not an argument. Use the annotation tools to flag any item promoted for being cheap, and ask BigAL to compare your ordering against the impact check rather than to estimate development time.\n\nA useful closing line reads: \"I moved Submit findability to the top and the typo to the bottom because findability blocked three of five from the learning goal while the typo blocked no one; I rejected the suggestion to do the quick fixes first, since that order would ship the same task-blocking defect again.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same prioritization discipline to triaging a course's redesign across a semester, where the issues are not interface defects but failing assignments, confusing readings, and a clunky gradebook. The temptation to fix the gradebook first because it is bounded is the same effort trap; the failing assignment that blocks the learning goal for most students has to lead. The structure transfers (severity times reach against the goal, access as a floor), but the units change from clicks to learning outcomes. A reader who sequences the redesign by what is administratively easiest has repeated the mistake of fixing the typo before the Submit button.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/05-07-prioritizing-iterations-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"05.07\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 05.07. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_prioritizing_iterations\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm your backlog is ordered by severity and reach against the learning goal, holds accessibility as a floor, and cites the evidence behind each item's position.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_prioritizing_iterations\", \"ail606_supplement_usability_testing_and_learning_evidence\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_5_7_prioritizing_iterations_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_prioritizing_iterations","stem":"A team plans to fix a welcome-screen typo and a button color first \"because they are quick,\" leaving a Submit-button findability issue that blocked 3 of 5 users for later. What is the prioritization error?","options":["Typos should never be fixed in usability iterations.","They ordered work by ease of fix rather than by severity and reach, so the task-blocking defect stays unaddressed.","Three of five is not enough to act on.","Button color is always the top priority."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Prioritization should weigh how much an issue blocks the goal and how many users hit it, not how easy it is to fix.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_5_7_prioritize_by_ease","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_prioritizing_iterations"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_5_7_prioritizing_iterations_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_prioritizing_iterations","stem":"Issue A blocked the core task for 3 of 5 users; Issue B was a cosmetic preference mentioned by 4 of 5. Which should rank higher and why?","options":["Issue B, because more participants mentioned it.","Issue A, because severity (it blocks the task) outweighs raw mention count for a cosmetic preference.","They tie, since severity and reach always cancel out.","Issue B, because cosmetic fixes improve satisfaction scores."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A frequent cosmetic comment does not outrank an issue that prevents users from completing the core task.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_5_7_frequency_over_severity","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_prioritizing_iterations"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_5_7_prioritizing_iterations_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"An AI tool ranks your usability issues by how often each keyword appears in session notes. What is the defensible boundary on using its ranking?","options":["Adopt the keyword-frequency ranking as your fix order.","Use it as one input, but re-rank by severity, reach, and access impact, since frequency of mention is not the same as severity.","Reject the ranking because AI cannot prioritize anything.","Fix issues in the exact order the AI lists them to stay objective."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Keyword frequency is a weak proxy for severity; the designer must re-rank using how much each issue blocks the learning goal.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_5_7_frequency_over_severity","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_5_7_prioritizing_iterations_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_prioritizing_iterations","stem":"A low-contrast button does not block the task but fails contrast guidelines for some learners. The team deprioritizes it below a typo. Why is that a defensible-reasoning problem?","options":["Contrast issues are purely aesthetic and can always wait.","An access barrier affecting some learners (a UDL variability concern) should not be ranked below a trivial cosmetic typo.","Typos are never worth fixing.","Contrast can only be judged by an automated tool, so it is unrankable."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Access barriers carry weight beyond task-blocking; deprioritizing them below trivial fixes ignores learner variability.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_5_7_ignore_access_barrier","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_prioritizing_iterations"]}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"065d5d1661d5f8bb1bddc8b2dfe2a2ee93c18511f1fd87c8dcdecc9be070c7b4","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}